# Task log — record what we did and why

`knowledge/task-log/` is the **durable story of completed work**: task, goal, why it mattered, what changed, how it works now, and especially **why this approach** (tradeoffs / rejected alternatives).

CHANGELOG = short “what the code does now.”  
PROBLEM_LOG = bug diagnosis.  
**Task log = full job narrative + reasoning.** All three can apply to one job.

## When (mandatory)

At the **end of every completed task** — parent session or specialist — that:

- Changed product code, config, CI, Docker, or security posture
- Closed or materially advanced a roadmap/security finding
- Produced an audit/ops conclusion future agents must not re-derive from scratch

Also required when **daddy** closes a multi-specialist dispatch (one aggregate entry is enough).

## When to skip

- Pure typo / formatting-only
- Aborted work with nothing learned
- Pure crack-index / “who owns X” with no decision
- Status-only poll (e.g. IBKR connected check) with no remediation

If skipping, Lifecycle must say `task_log=skipped` or `task_log=n/a` with a one-word reason in the report body when useful.

## How

1. Prefer scaffolder: `py -3 tools/task_log_new.py --slug <kebab> --title "<title>"`
2. Or copy `knowledge/task-log/_template.md` → `knowledge/task-log/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.md`
3. Fill **every** section — **Why this approach** is never optional for completed remediations
4. Prepend a row to `knowledge/task-log/INDEX.md` (newest first)
5. Cross-link from CHANGELOG / PROBLEM_LOG `Related:` when those files also get an entry
6. Lifecycle footer includes `task_log=<relative-path-from-repo-root>` or `skipped` / `n/a`

Example Lifecycle:

```text
**Lifecycle:** memory=changed | promotion=none | dashboard=clean | handoff=none | task_log=knowledge/task-log/2026-07-18-sec-001-008-remediation.md | problem_log=2026-07-18 — SEC-001–008
```

## Ownership

| Role | Duty |
|------|------|
| Parent / implementer | Write the entry for work they shipped |
| Daddy | Ensure one aggregate entry exists for the dispatch |
| Specialists | Write when their run is the source of truth (audit conclusion, ops diagnosis); otherwise hand facts to parent/daddy |
| Docs | May tidy prose; must not invent technical reasons |

## Anti-patterns

- ❌ “Fixed it” with no why / tradeoffs
- ❌ Secrets, tokens, or full `.env` contents in an entry
- ❌ Duplicating a 40-line stack trace (summarize)
- ❌ Skipping the task log because CHANGELOG already has a bullet
- ❌ Editing old entries to rewrite history — add a new dated note instead

